 ## Background
-The Sparse Radiance Field (SRF) was introduced in the context of the SPARF (Sparse Radiance Field) dataset and learning framework [S1]. The work builds on Plenoxels, which uses spherical harmonics for view-dependent radiance in voxel grids [S1]. The SPARF dataset contains one million SRFs at resolutions 32, 128, and 512, collected from 40,000 shapes across 13 classes [S1]. The method addresses the need for scalable learning of 3D radiance fields by leveraging sparsity and efficient optimization [S1].
+The Sparse Radiance Field (SRF) was introduced in the context of the SPARF (Sparse Radiance Fields) dataset and framework, which collected one million SRFs across multiple resolutions (32, 128, 512) for 13 object classes [S1]. The representation builds on the concept of Plenoxels, which replaced NeRF's MLP with spherical harmonics in a voxel grid, but extends it by explicitly managing sparsity via COO format to scale to large datasets and facilitate learning of 3D structures from few images [S1].
 
